Cambodian-Lao military leaders continue meeting to strengthen border security
The Cambodian side is led by General Ith Sarath, Deputy Commander-in-Chief, Joint Chiefs of Staff of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Chairman of the Commission for Security Cooperation along the Cambodia-Laos border, while the Lao side is led by Brigadier General Van Thorn Buktakvong, Deputy Chief of Staff. Lao People's Army and Chairman of the Commission for Cooperation and Maintenance of Order along the Lao-Cambodian Border.
On that occasion, General Ith Sarath said that on behalf of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and on behalf of the Commission for Security Cooperation along the Cambodia-Laos border, I would like to welcome and support the efforts of the two armies. This is the second meeting between the two armies to maintain order and security along the border between the two armies.
According to the General, the purpose of today's meeting is to communicate and promote security along the Cambodia-Laos border, as well as to continue the traditional cooperation from Yulong and to live in harmony with each other as good neighbors. Fences together.
In addition, General Ith Sarath ordered that the two commissions have no duty to resolve border issues, border affairs is the responsibility of the Joint Commission on Border Affairs of the two countries.
In order to successfully carry out the role of the two commissions, to make the border peaceful, the people to live in harmony, General Ith Sarath proposed and set the schedule to work together as follows:
First, we must all be willing to make our borders peaceful, friendly, united, cooperative and develop according to the aspirations of our leaders, including preventing any activities that undermine peace and the cases that occur must be resolved peacefully. And respect each other's sovereignty.
Second, to promote the relationship between our two armies in the spirit of friendship, solidarity, brotherhood and good neighborliness by creating sports, arts, entertainment, etc.
Third, to continue the working mechanism of our two commissions set out in the first meeting, especially the annual meeting, if necessary, as an extraordinary meeting. Every 6 months, if necessary, can hold an extraordinary meeting and set up a hotline for urgent communication.
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian National Anti-Drug Authority, the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) and the Australian Federal Police (AFP) have jointly opened a training workshop on money laundering investigations to strengthen their capacity. Cambodian law enforcement officers. This workshop is organized for 5 days from April 2 to 6, 2022.
The main purpose of the workshop was to identify money laundering identities and anti-money laundering mechanisms that convert illegal money, such as money from the drug trade, into legal money.
On that occasion, General Meas Virith, Secretary-General, thanked the UN and AFP for their cooperation in assisting Cambodia in the past, especially the organization of training seminars to strengthen the capacity of Cambodian law enforcement officers at this time. Has been working to increase the efficiency of the fight against money laundering and considered the work of money laundering investigation as a joint work that must be jointly prevented.
The General considered that this training course was another new knowledge for Cambodian law enforcement officers.
In addition, General Meas Virith stated that the drug problem is complicated and the results of these crimes are also used by criminals in various economic activities under the guise of money laundering to divert support for their activities. .
