Senior officials of the Ministry of Environment call on people to participate in the protection and conservation of mangrove trees and please participate in planting more for the sustainability of the environment and marine biodiversity
(Kampot): 1,000 mangrove seedlings were planted in Prek Tnaot fishing community area in Prek Tnaot commune, Bokor city, Kampot province this morning, April 30, 2022, in collaboration with the Ministry.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, who presided over the mangrove planting ceremony, called on the people, especially those in the protected areas, to participate in the protection and conservation of mangrove trees and to plant more mangrove trees for the sustainability of the environment. And marine biodiversity.
He considered that the activities of planting mangroves at this time show that we all consider environmental issues, especially climate change, consider the protection and maintenance of marine ecosystems and mangrove trees have played an important role in Protecting coastal residents from storms and mangroves contributes to the richness of biodiversity to sustain life.
Secretary of State Net Pheaktra emphasized that the conservation of mangroves and their associated ecosystems has been identified as a natural adaptation strategy and measures to mitigate the effects of climate change. Mangroves are a buffer zone between land and sea, counteracting the serious problems of coastal erosion and the threat of sea level rise. Mangrove forests help control the inflow of seawater into natural soils.
The Ambassador is proud that this great effort involves our friends and colleagues to improve the environment by planting trees and creating a legacy in honor of the 70th anniversary of The reign of Queen Elizabeth II, which will benefit the next generation and the friendship between Britain and Cambodia.
Mangrove forest is a great natural resource for mankind in general, especially the people who live and fish in the sea. In particular, according to the report of Prek Tnaot fishing community in Bokor, since the establishment of the fishing community since 2011, more than 50,000 mangrove trees have been planted on more than 120 hectares. In addition to mangroves, the palm forest fishing community has other seabed natural resources: seagrass and coral.
Mr. Ney Kong, Deputy Governor of Kampot Provincial Board of Governors, called on the community in Sangkat Prek Tnaot to participate in the protection and conservation of mangrove forests for the benefit of the community.
