In the spirit of high commitment to achieve gender equality, Cambodia plans to achieve 20% by 2024.
The project, supported by the Elsie Initiative, underscores the fact that increasing the percentage of women in the United Nations peacekeeping process is an important step in turning a commitment into action. Scientific and comprehensive estimates of the project will identify challenges or barriers, as well as specific opportunities for meaningful full participation of women in the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces.
The results as well as the recommendations received from the project will contribute to the formulation of policies and provide key guides in improving the institutional capacity to respond to identified or identified barriers and enhance participation. Full of meaningful women.
From a country with the United Nations Transitional Authority (UNTAC), Cambodia has become an active country in sending troops to participate in UN peacekeeping missions in Africa, the Middle East and Europe.
By leading by example, Cambodia is the second largest exporter of women peacekeepers among ASEAN member countries. With the implementation of this project, Cambodia aims to exceed the gender target of 11% by 2024, which is set out in the strategy to reduce the gender gap in the UN forces 2018-2028.
The fund is funded by the governments of Australia, Canada, Denmark, Finland, Germany, Norway, the Republic of Korea and the United Kingdom. The Elsie Initiative (EIF) supports the sustainable and meaningful full deployment and participation of peacekeepers through its funding.
