Crude oil prices continue to rise as supply fears linger over sanctions on Russia
LONDON (Reuters) - Oil prices continued to rise for a fourth straight day as of Friday, as fears over supply disruptions from Russia outweighed the closure of cities due to China's Kovid-19 virus. As the world's largest importer of crude oil. According to Reuters.
Oil prices remain volatile, with China showing no signs of easing sanctions, despite the impact on its economy and global supply chain.
"Oil market volatility will continue as the blockade spreads and extends into May and beyond, creating short-term risks for China's oil demand," he said. "Prices will fall."
On the supply side, OPEC + is likely to stick to its existing agreement and agree to another small output increase for June when the group meets on May 5. This is according to Reuters on Thursday.
However, a report from the Russian Ministry of Economy, Reuters, shows that Russia's oil production could fall by 17 percent this year as Western sanctions over Russia's aggression on Ukraine. Affect investment and exports.
Thai rice prices rise on demand from Middle East as Indian and Vietnamese rice prices remain unchanged
BANGKOK: Rice exports from Thailand rose this week on the back of rising demand from markets in the Middle East as rice prices from India and Vietnam remained unchanged. According to Reuters.
"There is an increase in demand from markets such as Iraq and Iran due to better relations with Saudi Arabia," said a Bangkok-based rice trader.
The weakening baht against the US dollar also contributed to the rise in rice prices this week, Reuters reported, citing traders.
India's 5 percent broken rice, the world's largest exporter, was quoted at $ 361 to $ 365 a tonne, unchanged from last week.
Vietnam's 5% broken rice sold at $ 415 a tonne on Thursday, unchanged from the previous week.
However, a trader in Ho Chi Minh City said prices could rise in the coming weeks due to low supply due to the end of the spring harvest. "Traders are reluctant to sign new export contracts pending higher prices," he said. This is according to a report by Reuters.
Officials: More than 200 billion juveniles and fish eggs flow into the Lower Mekong and Tonle Sap rivers each year
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Nao Thuok, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, confirmed that each year, about 200 billion fish fry and fish eggs flow into the Lower Mekong and Tonle Sap River, making Cambodia is rich in fish.
Dr. Nao Thuok pointed out that the evolution of these fish shows the importance of having a safe river that is connected to each other, especially the Mekong, Tonle Sap and three rivers. Including: Sekong River, Sesan River and Srepok River.
In addition, a senior official of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries also suggested that efforts be made to ensure that all rivers are safe for fish to travel.
In fact, this week, from April 23 to 27, a group of famous underwater explorers dived to a depth of 250 feet (about 75 meters) under the Mekong River in Cambodia.
This deep habitat is an important area for fish and livestock and may be the last habitat for the world's largest freshwater fish.
The area that scientists need to focus on is at the bottom of the Ramsar site, which is of international importance.
The area is covered by flooded forests, reefs, sand islands, streams, ponds, deep canyons and reefs and streams. The area is also home to more than 1,000 species of Mekong river fish, including two-faced pava, buffalo and big pra, which used to be the main food in Cambodia.
Researchers believe that these areas are important as habitats for fish in the dry season and as important spawning grounds, and may also be the last habitat for endangered giant fish. It is an area recognized as a special area and protected by local communities.
For 5 days, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia cracked down on 9 duty-free luxury cars nationwide
(Phnom Penh): For 5 days from April 25 to 29, 2022, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia cracked down on 9 duty-free luxury cars throughout the country, all of which took refuge under fake license plates. .
The nine duty-free vehicles seized by the General Department of Customs and the Cambodian Excise include:
Dr. Kun Nhim, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia through Fresh News, once called on car owners to take refuge under various license plates, please come and pay taxes for everyone and Deadline is June 30, 2022, but not 10% as before.
Dr. stressed that from July 1, 2022, tax-free vehicles that have been cracked down on will be required to pay taxes and duties, including fines, according to the regulations in force. Right-hand drive vehicles, which are prohibited from being imported in the event of a crackdown, will be demolished or dismantled as spare parts. With this, the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia cooperated with law enforcement forces to crack down on duty-free vehicles traveling on the road.
