Thailand plans to collect 300 baht tourism fee from foreign tourists in the third quarter of this year
BANGKOK: The Thai government plans to collect 300 baht from all foreign tourists traveling to Thailand between August and September this year using the Thai Tourism Fee (TTF) system. Performed by the airline. According to the Bangkok Post.
The airline raised concerns about the budget it will have to pay for the system and the additional manpower on the process, as the company is still struggling due to the effects of the epidemic and the low flow of passengers.
The Bangkok Post quoted Chote Trachu, permanent secretary of the Thai Ministry of Tourism and Sports, as saying: To raise funds from fees collected from foreign guests. The fund will be used to develop tourist sites and provide reassurance to tourists.
He said the tour fee was to help tourists in case of emergencies such as sudden illness, accidents or political unrest while traveling in Thailand.
The Bangkok Post reported that those exempted from the collection included tourists, diplomats, government officials, those with work permits from other countries under a memorandum of understanding with Thailand and children under the age of two.
Speaking at a meeting with the airline on Thursday, Mongkon said the airline must help screen tourists who pay the fee before issuing a ticket by submitting a final passenger list to a 15-day data link. Minutes after flight.
TTF system launches 30-day insurance for tourists when they go to Thailand.
He added that domestic and international airlines have been invited to register for the test run from May 2. The Ministry will provide training programs on the TTF system and implement pilot tests on the system. According to the Bangkok Post.
