WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The US economy contracted unexpectedly in the first quarter of this year as a surge in Kovid-19 cases disrupted economic activity. According to Reuters.
The Commerce Department reported Thursday that gross domestic product (GDP) fell 1.4 percent year-on-year last quarter. That was the first drop since the epidemic crisis in nearly two years. The US economy grew at a strong rate of 6.9% in the fourth quarter of last year.
Economists surveyed by Reuters forecast that the US economy will grow by 1.1% in the first quarter of this year. Estimates for the US economy ranged from 1.4 percent to 2.6 percent.
The slowdown in economic output reflects a larger trade deficit and lower levels of stockpile purchases.
Reuters reports that consumer spending is strong and businesses' investments in purchasing or upgrading production equipment have risen sharply. This helped boost domestic demand, excluding trade, inventories and government spending, which rose sharply from a 2.6 percent growth rate in the fourth quarter of 2021.
