China to reduce coal tariffs to zero from May 1
BEIJING, May 1 (Xinhua) - China will reduce import tariffs on all types of coal to zero percent from May 1, 2022 to March 31, 2023, the Ministry of Finance said on Thursday. Beijing is working to ensure energy security amid rising global prices and concerns over supply disruptions. According to Reuters.
Senior Chinese officials, including President Xi Jinping, have repeatedly stressed the important role of coal in China's energy mix, despite its commitment to climate change through climate change mitigation. Use coal gradually.
China will import 323.33 million tons of coal by 2021, accounting for about 8% of its total coal consumption.
However, the abolition of coal tariffs is seen as having little effect on China's coal purchases in 2022, as domestic output remains at a record high while import prices remain strong. Rising to historic highs. Some traders said the tariff reduction could benefit Chinese coal imports from Russia.
"Tariff reductions will make no difference to Indonesia's coal imports as tariffs are already zero," Reuters quoted a Beijing-based coal trader as saying. "And there is no impact on Australian coal due to the import ban."
"At that time, we could predict that it would benefit coal imports from Russia at a tariff rate of 6% for thermal coal," he added.
China's coal imports from Russia fell 30 percent in March from a year earlier, but in recent weeks some Chinese traders have begun asking for cheaper Russian goods after the European Union planned to impose sanctions. On this country. According to Reuters.
