Ukraine's militarization or disarmament and neutrality is a key Russian proposal that could allow Putin to use it as a propaganda campaign to declare victory over his war in Ukraine. That means the strongman of the Kremlin wants to take it and tell the Russian people and politicians, "Look! "Our military operations have eliminated the threat of NATO expansion into our southern border areas."
Indeed, for Putin, neutrality in Ukraine is only a small part of his ambitions, and most importantly, he wants Ukraine to remain under Russian influence forever, as he has publicly declared. The people of Russia and Ukraine are "one people."
In December 2021, Russia submitted security proposals to the United States and NATO, knowing in advance that the West would not be able to comply with them, and that Putin knew such a strategy would not succeed. No, on the international stage, but he wants to use it to justify the decision to wage war in Ukraine and to convince the Russian people that the United States and NATO are responsible for ignoring Russia's security proposals.
Ukraine has also sought to remain a neutral country after gaining independence from the former Soviet Union. Nevertheless, neutrality cannot be prevented by Russia, which in 2014 annexed Crimea and the Donbass Peninsula from Ukraine, and most recently, Putin's current war.
Zelensky has already stated that Ukraine may not join NATO, but Ukraine wants a guarantee from outside countries such as the United States, Britain or the European Union, which is committed to intervening directly to help Ukraine in the event of an attack from Russia in the future. But it depends on how brave Western leaders are in confronting Russia, as Putin may not be able to accept such a proposal.
