Commentary: Europe's destiny is in Europe's own hands
BEIJING, April 24 (Xinhua) - Macron, the current president of France, was re-elected president on April 24 local time. As a core member of the European Union, France is a key force in promoting European integration and adhering to strategic sovereignty. In the context of the resumption of war in Europe, Macron was re-elected President of France, receiving special attention.
In the campaign document, Macron said he would continue with the first-mandate policy to create a more independent nation. This gave him support, but it was also a problem Macron faced after being re-elected. It clearly shows the differences of interest in resolving the Ukraine issue with the United States.
This shows that under the abduction of the United States, Europe has become a major victim of the conflict between Russia and Ukraine. The United States, which is the cause of the Ukraine crisis, is standing by and watching the United States not only seize the opportunity to take advantage and put pressure on Russia, but also use the Ukrainian trap. Make Europe more dependent on America and follow America.
As a result, European cries calling for Europe's liberation from strategic dependence on the United States have become louder. In March, the European Union launched a "strategic compass plan" stating that it would create a European military force that differed from NATO in establishing its own European security framework, outlining the direction of the effort. The goal.
The fate of Europe must be in the hands of Europe itself. Macron's oath of allegiance to Europe should be implemented soon. The world needs a European strategic masterpiece, Europe must also add more stability and certainty to a world that is in turmoil and change.
The H3N8 bird flu virus has been known to spread since 2002, after first appearing in waterfowl in North America. It is known to spread to animals such as horses, dogs and seals, but has never been found to infect humans.
A 4-year-old boy in central China's Henan Province has been tested positive for the H3N8 bird flu virus, the National Health Commission (NHC) said in a statement on Tuesday. He was taken to hospital in early April with fever and other symptoms.
The NHC added that the boy's family raised chickens at home and lived in areas with large numbers of wild ducks. The boy contracted the disease directly from birds and it has not been found to have the ability to spread widely among humans. Tests on those who had close contact with the boy also found nothing wrong.
However, the NHC has warned the public to stay away from dead or sick birds and seek immediate medical attention if there are any fever or respiratory symptoms.
It should be noted that avian influenza occurs mainly in wild birds and poultry. Cases of human-to-human transmission are rare. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), "Avian influenza transmission from animals is due to direct contact with infected animals or contaminated environments, but does not cause widespread human-to-human transmission of the disease." That no. ”
