Ministry of Commerce: After joining the World Intellectual Property Organization for nearly 30 years, Cambodia has registered more than 80,000 trademarks
Cambodia joined WIPO in 1995 after committing to the adoption of a number of key laws, including national intellectual property laws and related regulations. After two years of participation, in 1997, the Royal Government of Cambodia decided to issue a sub-decree to establish the Department of Intellectual Property under the Ministry of Commerce.
On April 26, 2022, the Department of Intellectual Property, Ministry of Commerce, Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and WIPO organized the World Intellectual Property Day under the theme "Intellectual Property and Youth" Innovation for a Better Future ". Aeon Mall Sen Sok City (3rd floor) as well.
World Intellectual Property Day 2022 was presided over by Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce and Chairman of the National Intellectual Property Management Committee, with the participation of Ms. Dr. Phoeung Sakona, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts, Mr. Daren Tan, Director General of WIPO The US Ambassador to Cambodia represents the EU Ambassador to Cambodia and other stakeholders.
(Svay Rieng): Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, said that Svay Rieng is the first of 25 provincial capitals to be officially recognized to end the first open defecation (ODF). The back of the house in 2022.
This statement was made on the occasion that Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Foreign Affairs, presided over the official recognition ceremony of Svay Rieng province as "completed defecation" at the Svay Rieng Independence Monument on April 27, 2022.
"The Royal Government has launched a national strategic plan, a national action plan and a provincial action plan to achieve the goal of sustainable national development:" By 2025, all people in rural communities will have access to services, "he said. "Provide clean water and sustainable living in a hygienic environment."
In order to achieve the above goals, the Ministry of Rural Development has been implementing the National Strategic Plan on Water Supply, Rural Sanitation and Sanitation 2014-2025 and the Second National Action Plan on Rural Water Supply, Sanitation and Hygiene in 2014-2025. 2019-2023 in accordance with the Rectangular Strategy of the Royal Government.
According to the report of the Cambodian Socio-Economic Survey 2019-2020, in 2022, the population will have improved water resources by 80%, while the population will have improved access to toilets by 80% and households. About 89% use hand washing equipment in the home.
The Ministry of Rural Development has identified five target provinces: Kampong Speu, Kampong Chhnang, Kep, Prey Veng and Kandal, to complete open defecation by 2023.
Dr. Ouk Rabun also recommended that the Deputy Governors of the Capital and Provinces, relevant working groups, development partners, the private sector, authorities at all levels, specialized departments continue to mobilize all resources to support the rural water supply and sanitation sector to achieve the national strategic plan. Which is set to reach 90% by 2025 and 100% by 2030.
According to the Prime Minister, Samdech Techo Prime Minister has abolished the duty of wearing a mask throughout the country in public places, places or well-ventilated areas, and participated in the third dose of Kovid-19 vaccine to fight. And prevent the spread of Kovid-19 in order to continue to ensure the sustainability of community immunity and the exemption from the obligation to wear masks.
Following the report by Mr. TOch Poliva, Deputy Governor and Chairman of Svay Rieng Rural Water Supply and Sanitation Working Group on the progress of rural water supply and sanitation, Mr. Men Vibol, Governor of Svay Rieng, expressed his gratitude and congratulations for the achievements. In particular, the progress of the rural water supply and sanitation sector in cooperation with the working group of the Ministry and partner organizations and other private sectors.
Myanmar court sentences former leader Aung San Suu Kyi to five years in prison for corruption (Video inside) The civilian government, Aung San Suu Kyi, has been sentenced to five years in prison for corruption. This was released by Reuters on Wednesday, April 27, 2022.
The verdict is the first of a total of 11 charges against her involving corruption, each of which carries a maximum sentence of 15 years in prison. Wednesday's hearing was held without journalists, and Suu Kyi's lawyer was barred from speaking to reporters.
Aung San Suu Kyi was previously sentenced to six years in prison on charges of inciting anti-military insurgency, as well as violating COVID-19 principles and violating telecommunications laws. In addition to these charges, she was also charged with violating the National Secrecy Act.
