Minister of Culture: Youth as a catalyst for the creation and development of new cultural works, more creative and rich
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Pheung Sakona, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts, stated that when young people participate in society and know their values and rights, they will be a catalyst for the creation and development of new cultural works. Be more creative and rich.
The National Intellectual Property Day and Workshop is very useful and important, especially for young people, because it provides new knowledge as well as good experiences for practical implementation and dissemination at their local level to Contribute to the promotion of the creation of more new works.
"So when young people participate in society and know their values and rights, they will be a catalyst for the creation and development of new, more creative and diverse cultural works," he said. "On the basis of national identity, all of this will also be included in the presentations of national and international speakers for our national workshop this afternoon."
Cambodia is organizing the World Intellectual Property Day 2022 under the theme "Intellectual Property and Youth: Innovation for a Better Future" to help inspire young people to work together to create new technologies, new products, new works for Cambodia and the world.
In addition to geographical markers, the Ministry of Commerce has registered the Sambok Kampong Thom, Preah Vihear collective trademarks and plans to register more collective brands such as: Bak Spean, Kandal, Kroch, Pursat, Siem Reap and Battambang coconuts, which have contributed significantly to maintaining strong economic growth. Good governance and improve the living standards of the people.
Young people's increased understanding of intellectual property law can support the ambition of young people to acquire knowledge to innovate for a better future and also contribute to national economic development.
Please be informed that in the afternoon of the same day, the National Committee for the Management of Intellectual Property of Cambodia also organized a national workshop with national and international speakers to provide knowledge. Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth: Financial technology is changing the face of the entire financial industry to boost national economic growth
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Economy and Finance, stated that financial technology has been changing the face of the entire financial industry in the provision and access to financial services that actively participate and Strong in supporting and promoting national economic growth.
The speech of the General Doctor made in the National Consultative Workshop on the policy and direction of the financial technology sector in Cambodia on April 26, 2022.
Financial technology is changing the face of the financial industry as a whole in supporting and boosting economic growth through four major areas:
1. Financial technology can increase fair competition and increase the efficiency of financial services by reducing operating costs, saving time and improving the user experience.
2: Financial technology has the potential to create new markets that strengthen existing markets, in particular, to create a closer relationship and connection between financial activities and non-financial activities that have the potential to accelerate real transactions and operations. Online. This can not only create new market opportunities, but also provide the opportunity to create new sources of economic growth.
3. Financial technology can expand the financial environment as a driving force for poverty reduction by providing digital nurseries to provide and access formal financial services to the poor and inaccessible financial services.
4. Financial technology also plays an important role in strengthening and expanding small and medium enterprises (SMEs), which are important sources for creating and improving working conditions and promoting environmental and sustainable growth.
Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth continued that over the last decade, the Cambodian financial industry has achieved growth momentum and has become more involved in boosting the national economy by improving the financial environment, both in urban and rural areas. Rural areas, as a whole, are reflected in the dramatic increase in the number of microfinance institutions and payment service providers within the financial sector.
At the same time, various sub-sectors of the non-banking financial sector are also beginning to grow, with high potential to be exploited for boosting national economic growth. The establishment of a non-bank financial services authority also aims to establish a clear and sharp institutional mechanism to lead the development of this sector even more strongly.
