Indonesia's palm oil export ban puts world's biggest oil importers at a disadvantage
Mumbai: Global vegetable oil importers have no choice but to pay more for domestic supplies following Indonesia's shock ban on palm oil exports. The largest producer of palm oil in the world. According to Reuters.
The export ban has forced many plant importers to look for alternatives that are already in short supply due to bad weather and Russia's invasion of Ukraine.
Industry watchdogs have predicted that a ban by Indonesia from Thursday this week will push up prices for essential vegetable oils, including coconut oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil and rosemary. The rise will further strain importers, which are sensitive to prices in Asia and Africa, which are suffering from higher fuel and food prices.
Coconut oil, which is used to make everything from cakes and fries to cosmetics and cleaning products, accounts for nearly 60 percent of global vegetable oil exports, and Indonesia is the largest producer. The world's largest palm oil accounts for about a third of all vegetable oil exports. Indonesia announced the export ban on April 22 until further notice.
Indonesian President Joko Widodo says he wants to ensure the supply of food products in the country after global food inflation peaked after Russia's invasion of Ukraine. A large crop.
"The ban comes at a time when exports of all other essential oils are under pressure: Soybean oil is affected by the drought in South America, and crude oil is catastrophic," Fry said. "Canola in Canada and sunflower oil due to Russia's war on Ukraine."
Reuters reports that vegetable oil prices have risen by more than 50 percent in the last six months due to factors ranging from labor shortages in Malaysia to drought in Argentina. The largest exporter of soybean oil and Canada, the largest exporter of canola oil, cut supplies.
Major consumers hope that sunflowers from the largest exporter, Ukraine, will ease tensions, but supplies from Kiev have stalled due to what Russia calls "special operations" in the country.
Indonesia typically supplies nearly half of India's total palm oil imports, while Pakistan and Bangladesh import nearly 80 percent of their palm oil from Indonesia.
Heng Sour: Garment industry is doing well after Cambodia overcame Kovid 19 pandemic
(Phnom Penh): Heng Sour, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, confirmed that the garment and textile industry is recovering after Cambodia was able to control and prevent the spread of Kovid 19. In the first three months of 2022, exports of garments and textiles increased by about 15 percent to more than $ 3 billion.
Cambodia's garment and textile sector, as well as other sectors, experienced a crisis in 2020 and 2021 caused by the Kovid 19 epidemic. Many factories were closed and many workers lost their jobs.
In an interview with Fresh News, Mr. Heng Sour reminded that April 2021 is a difficult time in the field of garments and textiles. "In the last four months, we have had a lot of problems because 19 cases of Kovid have started to erupt in Cambodia, and the neighboring countries have also erupted, which has put pressure on our factories and orders have been disrupted," he said. Employees are also concerned. That makes it scary to work.
But now the working conditions in the garment and textile sector have improved after Cambodia was able to control Kovid 19 by developing resilience and immunity after Cambodia vaccinated its people at a high rate. This is according to Mr. Heng Sour.
He added that exports of garments, footwear and bags increased by nearly 15 percent to more than $ 3 billion in the first quarter of 2022.
Heng Sour emphasized, "This is a good growth, the expectation of our workers, and it is also a message to attract other investors to invest in Cambodia." "This increase makes investors who are looking for a production location and sustainability of the production chain look to Cambodia."
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training called the excellent results of the garment and textile sector, which is an essential engine of the Cambodian economy, a result of the management and launch of a proper plan to fight disease. Kovid 19 by the Royal Government of Cambodia led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
Mr. Heng Sour continued, "I would like to confirm that the vaccination campaign that Samdech Techo Hun Sen launched in 2021 is correct, which not only protects the lives of the people as well as the workers, but also protects the jobs and sources of income of our workers." "
Mr. Heng Sour also said that the Royal Government continues to work closely with market countries and relevant partners, including private investors, garment associations and unions to ensure that everyone is involved in Promote the garment and textile sector.
