Letter to readers: "Only the court can end the irrational protests and lack of logic of the former NagaWorld staff"
(Phnom Penh): So far, the resolution of labor disputes between NagaWorld and 200 former employees continues to drag on, despite negotiations to find a solution under the coordination of the Ministry of Labor several times.
A key obstacle to the talks was the union's demand for the company to accept the last 200 former employees who refused to return to work, while the employer refused to accept them. Absolutely work.
Now we try to understand the depth of this one labor dispute.
In general, in labor disputes, unions often demand that employers respect working conditions or require employers to provide benefits in accordance with the labor law, and especially when the employer terminates the employment contract must provide compensation and benefits in accordance with the labor law. Original. But what is strange, but this group of former employees of NagaWorld is still in Kranniel, demanding that the employer return to work after the company fired?
Such demands are seen as lacking in logic and seem to have an agenda behind them, and the points to consider are as follows:
"In our world, are there any workers who want to work in a place where they feel oppressed and useless?" The answer is no. So the case of the former NagaWorld is a strange thing and clearly there is something behind it.
Fourthly, the Freedom Park was established for the people, workers, and employees to express their opinions, but they refused to go there to protest, instead protesting at the company venue, which led to the disruption of the company's operations and seriously affect public order. Strong. Why do they do that? Is it a protest to find a work solution or is it an intention to cause annoyance and aggression?
Fifth, the Ministry of Labor has facilitated eight meetings to find a solution, but still no results, because each time the solution is obstructed in demanding that the company take the former employees back to work.
So it is likely that the last resort should be to send the case to court for a decision, as the arbitration and mediation mechanisms of the Ministry of Labor have been exhausted and remain unresolved. In this sense, only the court can make a final decision.
After all, without an agenda behind the protests, this protest would not have lasted until now. The patience of the competent authorities and the efforts of the Ministry of Labor to mediate have been limited, but there is still no solution due to the intention to cause obstruction by this group of former employees.
When the mediation mechanism is used to its full potential and still can not be found due to the inappropriate and unreasonable conditions of the claim, only legal action, the only judicial mechanism that can decide to close. Understand Business: The Royal Government and the Banking and Financial Institutions Plan to Release $ 150 Million in Low-Interest Loans to Recover Tourism Businesses
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia and the Banking and Financial Institutions plan to release $ 150 million to help tourism business owners after reopening the country through financing from the SME Bank. With low interest rates.
Mr. Lim Oun stated, "The total budget for the project is $ 150 million, of which $ 75 million comes from the Royal Government with SME Bank as the project implementation officer and another $ 75 million from the participation of banks as well as microfinance institutions." Stuff. ”
The CEO of SME Bank pointed out that the exact time for the release of the $ 150 million package is not yet set, but may be released in mid-June 2022 with a low interest rate of 6.5% per annum for the period. 7 years.
Mr. Lim Oun stated that the budget is to help and restore business opportunities in the tourism sector, such as hotels, guesthouses, food, beverage supplies and other services after Cambodia reopened the country after being affected by Kovid. -19.
Thuon Sinan, President of the Asia-Pacific Tourism Association of Cambodia, welcomed and was pleased with the good news, but with an interest rate of 6.5% per annum, he thought it was still a high rate for the affected tourism sector for more than two years. This.
He said that in the tourism sector, without the help of the government and the banking sector, this sector is very difficult to recover. The hotel has been closed for two years, so when it reopens, it costs a lot of money to buy new equipment.
