(Phnom Penh): Tourism experts have expressed concern over the lack of human resources in the tourism sector after more than two years, the tourism sector has suffered from the disease caused by Kovid 19 disease, causing human resources in this sector to abandon skills and He left his career to make a living.
Speaking at a roundtable discussion on "Cambodian tourism in the context of Kovid 19" organized by the Royal Academy of Cambodia on April 22, 2022, Ho Vandy, a representative of the Cambodian Tourism Association, said that during the Kovid 19 outbreak For more than two years, almost 80 percent of the tourism industry has abandoned the sector, while others have continued to borrow to continue their business.
He added that travel agencies have been affected by 19 cases of Kovid, which has led professionals to lose their jobs and skills, while others have given up their careers to pursue new careers. Income for life.
He added: "Indeed, the recent Khmer New Year has shown a great deal about the return to the revitalization of tourism. However, experts "Some tourism sectors can not come back on request because some professionals have already taken up other careers, so in the tourism sector we have lost a lot of professionals."
He added that Kovid 19 had devastated human resources, especially those in the tourism sector, who had been forced to give up their skills to pursue new careers, destroyed financial resources, and destroyed small and medium-sized tourism investment projects. Large, but the Ministry of Tourism, the Royal Academy of Cambodia, as well as all relevant partners have made great efforts to call back human resources to re-engage in this profession.
Ms. Chhay Sivlin, President of the Association of Travel Agents of Cambodia, also said that the full opening of the country, openness to foreign tourists, combined with the increase in tourists, is a motivating factor for tourism professionals to return to work. According to their knowledge and skills.
"However, some tourism professionals will not return because they have acquired new skills and new occupations," she said. However, there is still hope that tourism professionals will return to capture their skills by seeing the growing number of local tourists, combined with their existing skills.
