(Kampot): The road use dispute between Ms. Min Ko Anna and Ms. Men Bunthoeun in Kampong Kreng village-commune, Teuk Chhou district, Kampot province has not been closed yet, despite the mediation of the provincial governor. To this day, Ms. Men Bunthoeun continues to demand the use of the road within the premises of Ms. Min Ko Ana, although she has already made a new road into her home.
Disputes over land use between Min Ko Anna and Men Bunthoeun have been gaining traction on social media. This dispute has been resolved and mediated by the Kampot provincial authorities many times, but has not yet been resolved.
On the night of February 18, 2022, Mr. Mao Thunin, Governor of Kampot Province, also went to mediate to find a solution to the dispute on the use of this road. To find out the correct use of this road, the provincial governor also set up a working group to inspect and measure the disputed land. On February 19, the team also went to measure the land of Ms. Min Ko Anna and Ms. Men Bunthoeun.
According to the survey, the team has not been able to determine the size of the land of Ms. Men Bunthoeun and her daughter Hak Lina because the land owner did not provide enough documents.
Ms. Min Ko Ana expressed her desire for the dispute to be resolved as soon as possible, not wanting it to linger too long, so that she could enjoy living and developing tourism services on her land.
"For me, I respect the law, as well as the expertise of the authorities and the actions of the provincial governor," she said. So, if you have any action, I accept everything according to the right.
Mao Thunin, the provincial governor, who is well-known for resolving out-of-court disputes, said he had not yet dropped mediation and had set up an expert commission to find out the truth about the land dispute. Well.
Mr. Mao Thunin acknowledged that the land dispute was a bit difficult to resolve because each party was determined to win and lose and rely on intervention to resolve the dispute has not yet reached a compromise.
So far, despite still appealing to the Kampot Provincial Court to demand the use of this road, Ms. Men Bunthoeun has already prepared a new road to enter her land.
Please note that the location of the road is in dispute above, both Ms. Min Ko Anna and Ms. Men Bunthoeun are land that has not been registered yet.
Pursuant to the spirit of the Inter-Ministerial Proclamation No. 02 PRK.03 dated 26 November 2003 between the Ministry of Justice and the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction on the determination of the duties of the court and the Cadastral Commission related to land disputes, divided into disputes The two lands include:
1: Land disputes that have already been registered, that is, land with all kinds of title deeds issued by the Cadastral Administration must be under the jurisdiction of the court 2: Land disputes that have not yet been registered in the cadastral If you do not have any title deed issued by the Cadastral Administration, it is under the jurisdiction of the Cadastral Commission.
In the case of the above land dispute, the Cadastral Commission has the mission to resolve land disputes (unlicensed land issued by the Cadastral Administration) which is not yet registered in the cadastral area outside the jurisdiction or in the jurisdiction area which cannot be mediated by the administrative committee. And is not a land dispute involving a claim for division of inheritance or a dispute over a contract of sale, lease or mortgage.
The Cadastral Commission resolves each land dispute, starting from the district level to the provincial capital level and finally to the National Cadastral Commission.
