China's central bank pushes for more support for direct-to-consumer services
BEIJING (Reuters) - China's central bank and insurance regulator are urging financial institutions to step up support for the services sector, which relies heavily on direct contacts and small companies affected by the Kovid-19 outbreak.
Reuters reports, citing a central bank statement, that financial support should be given priority to key sectors, including transportation and consumer goods.
The statement said that flexible financial measures should be made to provide mortgages for homebuyers affected by the Kovid outbreak.
According to Reuters, the People's Bank of China will reduce the amount of cash held by banks as reserves, the first reduction this year. The measure will help release about 530 billion yuan ($ 83.25 billion) into long-term liquidity to boost a slowing economy and take effect on April 25.
In a statement, the People's Bank of China said the effectiveness of the policy would depend on monetary adjustments being fully transferred to the economy to deliver benefits as soon as possible.
China's gross domestic product (GDP) grew 4.8 percent in the first quarter from a year earlier, but a sharp drop in key economic data in March put risks to the outlook for the economy. The second largest of this world. According to Reuters.
