Sri Lanka Says IMF to Consider Urgent Assistance Request to Solve Domestic Financial Crisis
The International Monetary Fund (IMF) will consider providing quick financial assistance to its heavily indebted country after mediation from India, the Sri Lankan Ministry of Finance said on Tuesday. According to Reuters.
A delegation led by Sri Lankan Finance Minister Ali Sabry began formal talks with the IMF in Washington on Monday on a program the Sri Lankan government hopes will help inject its foreign exchange reserves and attract finance. Temporary relief to pay for imports of essential goods such as fuel, food and medicine.
The country's devastating financial crisis comes as the effects of Kovid-19 worsen on mismanagement of government finances and rising fuel prices have reduced reserves. Foreign currency. Fuel, energy, food and medicine have been low for weeks.
"Sri Lanka is seeking $ 3 billion in the coming months from various sources, including the International Monetary Fund, the World Bank and India," Sabry told Reuters earlier this month.
