China's economy grows in Q1 but recurrence of Kovi outbreak affects economic outlook
The economy of the world's second-largest economy lost momentum in the second half of last year due to a slump in real estate and a crackdown on regulation that slowed growth, the AFP news agency reported.
China's National Bureau of Statistics (NBS) said first-quarter economic growth exceeded expectations by 4.8 percent year-on-year. Costs during Chinese New Year and factory production increase.
However, the Chinese economy seems to be in trouble in the near future with the introduction of Beijing's Kovid Zero measures to curb outbreaks that have caused supply chain disruptions and shutdowns. Tens of millions, including in the economic hubs of Shanghai, Shenzhen and Jilin, which are grain markets in the northeast.
Restrictions on the virus in March hit retail sales as consumers shunned shopping and pushed unemployment higher.
The Kovid-19 pandemic, as well as sanctions on Russia's economy, increase the risk for Chinese officials to achieve Beijing's full-year growth target of about 5.5 percent.
NBS data showed that industrial production growth slowed to 5 percent in March, down from January and February. Meanwhile, retail sales fell 3.5 percent and the city's unemployment rate hit a 22-month high of 5.8 percent last month. According to the AFP news agency.
