Sri Lanka declares failure to repay foreign debt for first time in history
(Sri Lanka) - Sri Lanka announced on Tuesday that with extremely low foreign exchange reserves, the countries affected by the crisis will fail to repay their foreign public debt pending a bailout package from the International Monetary Fund (IMF). ). According to the Kyoto News Agency.
This is the first time an island nation in South Asia has declared a debt default since gaining independence from Britain in 1948.
The crisis caused by the default's debt is likely to be due to the effects of the Kovid-19 pandemic, which has hit Sri Lanka's tourism sector hard and the so-called debt trap involving large-scale borrowing. China for unsustainable infrastructure projects.
"Sri Lanka has a good record of foreign debt service since independence in 1948," the Kyoto News Agency quoted a press release as saying from the Sri Lankan Ministry of Finance.
"However, recent events, including the effects of the Kovid-19 pandemic and the effects of hostilities in Ukraine, have devastated Sri Lanka's fiscal situation, which has led to continued delays," the statement said. "External public debt service is not possible."
According to the Sri Lankan Ministry of Finance, Sri Lanka's policy of suspending debt service will apply to all international bonds, all bilateral loans, excluding swaps between the central bank and the bank. All foreign ventures and loans with commercial banks and institutional lenders.
The Sri Lankan Ministry of Finance says the IMF has assessed Sri Lanka's foreign debt as unsustainable and that repayment of foreign debt is no longer a viable policy.
"The government intends to resume its discussions with the IMF as soon as possible in order to establish and present to the country's creditors a comprehensive plan for the recovery of public debt," the statement said. "The outside of Sri Lanka is in a fully sustainable position." According to the Kyoto News Agency.
