China's trade with Russia slows but remains higher than total trade growth
BEIJING: China's total trade with Russia rose more than 12 percent in March from a year earlier, down from February this year but still outpaced China's overall trade growth. According to Reuters.
Exports to and from Russia rose 12.76 percent in March to $ 11.67 billion, down Reuters, citing Chinese customs data released on Wednesday. From the 25.7% increase achieved in February when Russia launched its invasion.
However, the growth in March was higher than the 7.75% increase in China's total trade with all countries and regions, which totaled $ 504.79 billion in March.
Last year, total trade between China and Russia rose 35.8 percent to 146.9 billion US dollars, the highest ever, according to Reuters.
As sanctions against Russia increase, China could help offset some of the impact on Russia by buying more goods from the neighboring country. But analysts say they have yet to see any major signs that China is violating Western sanctions on Russia.
"China's economic and trade cooperation with other countries, including Russia and Ukraine, remains normal," Chinese customs spokesman Li Kuiwen told a news conference.
In the first quarter, China's trade with Russia increased by 30.455% compared to the same period last year. Russia is an important source of oil, gas, coal and agricultural commodities for China. According to Reuters.
