New mechanisms needed to tackle debt as poor countries hit by rising consumer prices
WASHINGTON: The International Monetary Fund (IMF) said on Monday that rising global food and energy prices have risen sharply as the war in Ukraine is hitting poor countries and better mechanisms to deal with debt tensions. The government will need to prevent debt repayment.
"With the risk of rising public debt and the reversal of financial constraints amid policy concerns, a global cooperation approach is needed to move forward," the two senior IMF officials said. "Troubleshooting debt in an orderly manner and preventing failure to repay unnecessary debt."
Rising food and energy prices are hitting low-income countries and they may need more aid and higher concessional financing, according to Reuters. Countries should reform to improve debt transparency and strengthen debt management policies to reduce risks.
The two senior IMF officials added that about 60 percent of low-income countries were already in or facing debt tensions. Rising interest rates in large economies could lead to higher borrowing costs.
The two officials said the credit crunch was exacerbated by a slowdown in foreign lending from China, which is facing concerns over the ability to repay debt in the real estate sector, closures due to Kovid-19 and Problems with existing loans provided to developing countries.
