Mr. HUN Manet: The signing of the Memorandum of Understanding between Prince Bank and TYDA is a humanitarian engagement that provides hope, smiles and happiness to the people of Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Through the Memorandum of Understanding between the Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors Association (TYDA) and Prince Bank MKU, Mr. HUN Manet, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the TYDA Association, expressed optimism that the signing of the MoU is a joint venture. Humanitarian work to give people hope, smiles and happiness.
The Memorandum of Understanding was signed by Mr. HUN Manet, Chairman of the Board of Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors Association, and Mr. HUN Surachana, Managing Director and Chief Executive Officer of Prince Bank, with the participation of Ms. Pech Chanmony, Vice Chairman of the Board. Samdech Techo Youth Volunteer Doctor at Phnom Penh Hotel on April 12, 2022.
The MoU aims to provide additional support for programs and activities in the field of public health care to provide free medical check-ups to people across the country.
Mr. Hun Manet also said that "the signing of the memorandum now is the cause of everyone working together to do humanitarian work to give hope, smile and happiness to the people."
The Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Association stated that there is nothing bigger than life, health, family well-being that everyone must participate to help the people and demand the participation of the private sector and related sectors.
Mr. HUN Manet continued that until now, the Royal Government has managed the situation of Kovid-19 better and better, all of which came from the contribution of the private sector.
He expressed the hope that Prince Bank will continue to support the Samdech Techo Young Volunteer Doctors Association (TYDA) to provide opportunities, opportunities and capacity building to members and working groups to help Cambodians in need.
It should be noted that as of today, TYDA has examined and treated 336,353 poor people nationwide and 6,153 critically ill patients have been sent to Phnom Penh for further treatment and with their partners. Also, 56 serious heart patients were treated for free.
