BANGKOK: The Russian-Ukrainian war is pushing up domestic steel prices, which have already risen by 20 percent, leaving home buyers and construction companies with higher costs. According to the Bangkok Post.
Steel was among the raw materials that experienced higher prices after a conflict erupted in February that pushed global oil prices higher. Russia is a world-class steel producer and exporter, but since the invasion of Ukraine, steel production in Russia and Ukraine has been suspended or shut down.
Real estate developers can shift this burden to new buyers who now have to pay more when buying a house or condo.
The Bangkok Post reported that businesses affected by rising steel prices have not yet discussed measures to combat the rise. Steelmakers are currently facing high energy costs and are addressing issues by increasing production efficiency and reducing costs.
