Russia's foreign currency debt settlement "partially failed"
The AFP news agency reported, citing a statement issued by S&P, that Western sanctions on Moscow over its invasion of Ukraine were likely to increase and hinder "the will and technical capacity." Russia in respect of its debt obligations to foreign creditors.
Russia paid interest on some debt obligations in March after the US Treasury Department allowed Russia to use its foreign currency to pay off debts outside Russia.
But the United States on Monday reversed that decision and only allowed Russia to repay its debt using the dollars in US banks.
Russia's Finance Ministry on Wednesday announced that it had paid off nearly 650 million US dollars in debt, due on April 4, according to AFP.
S&P's "partial failure" debt rating occurs when a borrower fails to meet certain debt obligations but is still able to repay other debts on time.
As in other countries, Russia borrows in the form of debt obligations, often in dollars, and has to pay interest on a regular basis in addition to repaying those debts.
A country is considered a failure to repay its debt when it fails to fulfill its commitments to creditors, which may be governments or institutions such as the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank or financial market investors. According to the AFP news agency.
