The war dragged down more than 45% of Ukraine's economy, while Russia's economy fell by more than 11%
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - Ukraine's economy is likely to plummet to 45.1 percent this year as Russia's aggression closes exports, cuts exports and boosts activity, the World Bank (WB) says. The economy cannot function in large parts of the country. According to Reuters.
The World Bank also forecast that Russia's economy will shrink by 11.2 percent in 2022 due to financial sanctions by the United States and its Western allies on Russia's banks, state-owned enterprises and other institutions.
For Ukraine, the World Bank estimates that for Ukraine, more than half of the country's business is closed while other businesses are operating well below normal capacity, Reuters reported. . The closure of the Black Sea shipments from Ukraine cut about 90% of the country's grain exports and half of its total exports.
The World Bank says the war has disrupted economic activity in many areas and disrupted agricultural operations.
Estimates of infrastructure damage, previously estimated at more than $ 100 billion as of early March, or about two-thirds of Ukraine's 2019 gross domestic product, could be higher "as the war continues. "Go ahead and cause more damage."
(Sri Lanka) - Sri Lanka will need about $ 3 billion in external assistance over the next six months to help restore supplies of essential goods, including fuel and medicine, the country's finance minister has said. According to Reuters.
Reflecting on the search for $ 3 billion to be used temporarily to address domestic needs as the country prepares for talks with the International Monetary Fund (IMF) this month, Finance Minister Ali Sabry said. "It's a very difficult task," he said in his first interview since taking office this week.
Reuters reports that Sri Lanka will look to restructure its international state bonds and seek a moratorium on debt repayments, and the country is confident it can negotiate with creditors on repayments. $ 1 billion due in July this year.
Sri Lanka has $ 12.55 billion in international public debt and $ 1.93 billion in international currency reserves at the end of March, according to central bank data.
Sabry added that he would lead a delegation of Sri Lankan officials to Washington to begin talks with the IMF on April 18 and that a financial and legal adviser would be selected within 21 days to assist the government. To restructure its international debt.
"When we meet them, the first thing is that there is confidence in the entire international monetary community that we really want to do it," he said. We are transparent, we are willing to participate. ”
