Conservatives want to review relevant articles of the law to severely punish those with hand traps and escorts
Want to review the relevant articles of the law to increase the penalties and severe fines for those who have traps in hand and escort dogs to catch wild animals.
Mr. Seng Teak confirmed this during the opening ceremony of the Zero Trap Campaign in Stung Treng Province on April 7, 2022, which lasted for one month.
In addition, Mr. Seng Teak also suggested setting up a mobile joint law enforcement team in the target provinces of the Zero Trap campaign to inspect wildlife markets in provincial towns, districts, communes, restaurants, hotels and tourist resorts.
As a partner organization that has been actively involved with the Ministry of Environment, he has fully supported the initiative to organize a zero trapping campaign in the Cambodian Protected Areas by the Ministry of Environment in collaboration with relevant NGO partners.
Mr. Seng Teak added that traps are the main cause of the loss of important wildlife in Cambodia, such as big tigers and traps, leading to the rapid and rapid decline of some wildlife species such as: leopards, tigers, clouds, cats, swords. Wild dogs, bears and lizards, etc.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, in implementing the law of the Ministry of Environment to prevent natural resource crimes and wildlife crimes in 2021, the Ministry of Environment and its partner organizations have withdrawn. A total of 61,611 traps from 73 protected areas and 3 biodiversity corridors in Cambodia.
According to the senior official, Cambodia's wildlife resources are at high risk due to the ongoing hunting and trapping crisis caused by the illegal use and trafficking of wildlife. Without immediate action, Cambodia will face the loss of its priceless wildlife resources in the near future.
The Ministry of Environment calls on all people to stop eating wildlife meat and buy products from other wildlife. And I urge all people in Cambodia to say no to wildlife and participate in the conservation of natural resources of the Kingdom of Cambodia to be sustainable.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that in the efforts to prevent natural resource crime, including deforestation and wildlife crime, the Ministry of Environment has been cooperating with development partners and conservation partners to create a local economy to change the habits of some people. There is a habit of going into the forest to cut down trees and hunt wild animals for new options, new occupations for people living around and in protected areas.
According to a 2020 report by WWF on the Silence Trap and Trap Crisis in Southeast Asia, an estimated 12.3 million traps are endangering wildlife in 11 protected areas in Indonesia, Malaysia. Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia.
The report also states that more than 230,000 traps have been removed from five protected areas in Cambodia in the past nine years (2010-2019). These protected areas include the Cardamom Range, Kulen Promtep Sanctuary, Keo Sima Sanctuary, Sre Pok Sanctuary and Phnom Prech Sanctuary.
