PARIS (Reuters) - The first round of French presidential elections began this Sunday morning (Paris time) after a quiet election campaign as the whole of Europe focused on the war in Ukraine. This was released by the AFP news agency on Sunday, April 10, 2022.
Polling stations across the country are open to allow more than 48.7 million French voters to register to cast their ballots for 12 presidential candidates, including caretaker President Macron. Who is seeking to continue in his second term.
It should be noted that in the first round of the presidential election, the French people will select only two candidates who will advance to the next round, the second round of elections, which will take place on April 24. According to opinion polls, Makrong and Marin Le Pen have the highest percentage of candidates in the second round, with the first round of voting results expected to be released at 8 p.m. Sunday (Paris time).
