The European Union (EU) has launched a forum for EU countries to jointly purchase gas and liquefied natural gas as they try to reduce their dependence on Russian fuel and create a platform to counter supply risks. . According to Reuters.
Ukraine's aggression by Russia, Europe's largest gas supplier, has pushed already high energy prices to record highs, prompting the EU to reduce its reliance on Russia. Russia's fossil fuels by increasing imports from other countries and rapidly expanding renewable energy.
EU leaders approved a joint gas purchase plan at a summit last month in which member states, including Spain and Greece, called for a bailout, Reuters reported. Against possible supply disruptions. Russia supplies 40% of the EU's total gas demand.
The newly formed forum commission and representatives of EU member states met on Thursday and will gather countries' demands and facilitate talks with major gas and natural gas suppliers.
Reuters reports, citing a statement issued by the new commission, that the plan is voluntary and countries have no obligation to participate. The plan aims to use the power of the EU as the world's largest buyer of gas to attract supply at a stable price that reflects the predictability and size of the EU market as a whole.
European gas prices hit record high after Russia launches what it calls a "special military operation" in Ukraine, and analysts warn that Europe's rush to buy gas does not. Yes, Russia in large quantities can make poor countries difficult to supply the gas they need.
The European Union (EU) has promised to stop using Russian fuel by 2027 and will unveil a detailed plan in May to implement it. According to Reuters.
