Q1: The Russian army has launched an operation to invade Ukraine, which has so far lasted almost 50 days and is still unfinished. Can you confirm the real reason why Russia sent troops to attack Ukraine?
Further expansion of the North Atlantic Alliance infrastructure or any attempt to gain a military position on Ukrainian territory is unacceptable. One problem is that in the territory bordering Russia, anti-Russian hostilities are emerging. With complete control from the outside, they do everything to attract NATO armed forces and acquire modern weapons.
For the United States and its allies, this is a policy of restraining Russia with tangible geopolitical dividends. For our country, this is a matter of life and death, a focal point of our historical future as a nation. This is not only a real threat to our interests, but to the life and sovereignty of the state.
Since 2015, Kiev authorities have not complied with the Minsk Agreement on a Peaceful Crisis, which was ratified by the United Nations, and finally late last year refused to implement the document. They also began implementing plans to become members of NATO.
We have no plans to occupy Ukraine. We do not intend to place a burden on anyone by force. We have no intention of violating the interests of Ukraine and the Ukrainian people. These events are related to the protection of Russian territory from those who have taken Ukraine hostage and are trying to use it against our country and our people.
A: President Putin has decided to launch a special military operation to protect the people who have suffered from the abuses and genocide of the Kiev regime for eight years. It is clear that if Russia fully attacks Ukraine, it will cause many civilian casualties. Our troops are commanded to use weapons to destroy military infrastructure. Even Ukrainian military bases have not been targeted.
Q3) Can you tell us what Russia wants from Ukraine during the negotiations to end the war and withdraw its troops from Ukraine?
Kiev authorities announced that they were ready to negotiate with Russia and submitted the following provisions of the future agreement: refusal to become a member of NATO; Duty to conduct military exercises with the consent of the guarantor of Russia. Russia agreed to withdraw some troops from Kiev.
Q4) This is the first time I have seen joint ventures by the United States and its allies, as well as hundreds of thousands of other private companies, and I believe that Such strong sanctions are also an experiment for the leaders of other countries to learn from. In this regard, is Russia seriously affected by the sanctions, or is Russia ready for such actions?
A: Some Western countries have imposed new sanctions against the Russian Federation due to the situation around Ukraine. We see that accusations, intimidation and threats are the only tools that the West has always used. This can not be used against superpowers, especially Russia and other major international actors.
Sanctions imposed for any reason without a UN Security Council resolution are typical of the United States. According to Dmitry Peskov, a spokesman for the Russian president, Western sanctions were severe and Russia was prepared.
We clearly understand that new sanctions and sanctions against us will exist and that Russia's military operation in Ukraine is an excuse for the West to impose more sanctions.
This is not the first time we have faced sanctions. We have all the potential to develop ourselves. The Russian government has begun to support the people and the economy by reducing the use of the dollar in foreign exchange reserves, shifting to non-Western capital markets and protecting investment projects from negative external influences.
