Senior officials of the Ministry of Public Works request companies that have prizes on the award-winning products to have helmets to encourage more people to wear helmets
(Phnom Penh): A senior official of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport has asked companies that have incentives to put on their products to help put on helmets as a reward to encourage people to wear more helmets.
Ms. Mon Manvi, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Secretary-General of the National Road Safety Committee (NCDC), made the remarks at a workshop with stakeholders on the possibility of providing quality helmets. Good for Cambodian workers and children.
A senior official of the Ministry of Public Works said that many private companies have provided assistance to the Royal Government in the event of natural disasters, and some of the company's products have been rewarded. She expects that they will also be able to provide such incentives as helmets, as this will help the Royal Government to prevent deaths due to traffic accidents.
Kim Panha, AIP Country Director for Cambodia, said that traffic accidents are also a major concern for Cambodian children and workers, especially those working in the garment and footwear sector. 800,000 people at a time.
Of the 2,152 victims who died in road accidents in 2019, nearly 40 percent were children and workers, he said. Most of the victims were motorcyclists and more than 70% were not wearing helmets at the time of the accident.
Due to such important helmets, Ms. Men Manvi and Mr. Kim Panha both expressed the same willingness for private companies to provide good quality helmets to Cambodian workers and children.
On that occasion, Mr. Chhay Sophal, Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Information, suggested a one-hour national movement and other programs to commemorate those who died in traffic accidents to raise public awareness. He also donated 20 helmets to AIP. Commentary: Arms sales to Taiwan and US reliance on independence are acts of disregard for Taiwanese security
