(Stung Treng): Speaking from Stung Treng Province, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, outlined seven key points for the Zero Trap Campaign, which is the expectation of the Ministry of Environment as well as partner organizations within the framework of Zero Trap Campaign in Protected Areas for 6 months (March to October 2022) and a new expectation of protecting Cambodia's wildlife for future generations.
* First, the public will be aware of the negative effects of traps on wildlife, and this trap is not only detrimental to wildlife, but can also be dangerous to humans.
* Second: To let the public know that eating wild animals is an incentive for hunters to continue to set traps, continue to hunt, continue to create traps that are more dangerous, such as traps to break the legs, shock traps and so on.
* Third: To awaken those who like to eat wild animals, consider that if there is no demand, there is no supply.
* Fourth: To educate as a whole that the species caught, sold or eaten for a small price, but if left to live in the wild, it will be of great value to our biodiversity for science. Learn knowledge for generations to come.
* Fifth: Within the legal framework, in order for us to examine what problems lead to the continued occurrence of trapping, in that situation, we should look at additional laws or conditions so that we can reach prevention. Get or reduce as much as possible to the activities and as a warning to those who commit crimes to repel.
* Sixth: To know that eating meat has no health benefits, science has not confirmed that any meat or bone can cure any disease.
And 7: Encourage all parties to work together to prevent and avoid traps, even though we know our tradition is past, but we strive to provide new options and save wildlife. In this forest, which is important not only for biodiversity, but also as a viable source of income from tourism, which is an inexhaustible source of income for communities living in protected areas.
