(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT) issued a proclamation on April 7, 2022, stating that it will not allow cars to be released or deformed to check and make license plates.
According to a statement obtained by Fresh News, in order to ensure security, order, safety and control of traffic vehicles on the roads, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport will not allow access to technical inspections or registration. And do not equip vehicle license plates for vehicles that have been modified to install the wrong car model without permission from the Ministry.
According to the announcement of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, in order to ensure the security, order, safety and control of traffic vehicles on the roads, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to all car owners and owners of all types of car repair garages:
* First, modified vehicles, car models that affect the safety of the vehicle, such as: steering system, traction system and chassis and without permission from the Ministry of Public Works and Transport is not allowed to inspect the technical specifications or registration. Provide identification and vehicle identification plates.
* Second, the car assembly and repair garage that carries out the car modeling activities that affect the safety of the vehicle and did not apply for the car repair permit, the Ministry of Public Works and Transport will take action to close and suspend business activities at the garage location.
The following is the announcement of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport: Cambodia exported more than 1.5 million tons of cassava in the first quarter
Mr. Chhim Vachira, Director of the Battambang Provincial Department of Agriculture, said that the cassava crop has a good market situation, while the Thai side orders daily, exporting nearly 3,000 tons of cassava per day.
He said that in 2021, cassava crops will face few problems due to floods, but in terms of yield, there will be an increase due to more cultivated area than in 2020. It is estimated that domestic consumption of cassava is only about 30-35 percent, with Cambodia exporting to neighboring countries.
This national policy aims to make Cambodia a location for production, processing and a reliable supplier of cassava products to boost exports to the world market.
This policy also has three main goals:
First, to change from traditional or family cassava production to commercial cassava production; Second, to support potential cassava processors and attract investment to process high value-added cassava products to supply the market. 3. Enhancing trade competitiveness by penetrating and capturing market share by linking the inter-sectoral sectors involved in exports.
In the world, cassava is recognized as a 21st century crop, grown from family to large-scale export to the food industry and ethanol production.
