World Bank cuts East Asia and Pacific economic forecasts over Ukraine war
MANILA: The World Bank (WB) has cut its growth forecast for East Asia and the Pacific for 2022 due to the economic impact of Russia's invasion of Ukraine. According to Reuters.
The World Bank has also warned that the region could lose further momentum if the situation worsens.
The Washington-based bank expects 2022 economic growth in East Asia and the Pacific (EAP), including developing countries, according to a World Bank report released Tuesday. China will also grow 5 percent, lower than the 5.4 percent forecast in October.
But the World Bank said growth could slow to 4 percent if the situation worsens and governments' policy responses become weaker.
Noting the Chinese government's ability to provide stimulus measures to offset the negative impact, the bank said China's economy is expected to grow by 5% this year, down from previous estimates. 5.4%.
