2: To promote the consistent and rigorous implementation of the measures issued by the Royal Government to prevent the spread of Kovid-19, especially the measures "3 prevent and 3 do not" and strengthen the implementation. Tourism safety rules and minimum operating standards (SOP) procedures along the new normal path to maintain business stability and ensure the safety of national and international tourists.
4. Encourage business owners and tourist destinations to organize incentives for vaccinated tourists, while all staff must be vaccinated according to the Ministry of Health.
5. To cooperate with traders and tour operators to improve the quality of tourism products, maintain the environment-hygiene and maintain the prices of products and services appropriately. All accommodation, food and other services must be clearly written on the table to inform guests in advance to avoid excessive fees. In case of non-compliance with the posted schedule, the business owner shall be liable before the law in force.
6: To set up a working group to disseminate and promote the implementation of the contents of this circular in a comprehensive and comprehensive manner to tour operators and to make a report summarizing the statistics of tourists at various resorts and tourist destinations and send it to the Ministry of Tourism. Be on time every day. Pan Sosak Meets New Zealand Deputy Minister of Commerce on Trade Strengthening and Expansion
(Phnom Penh): This morning, April 5, 2022, Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, led a delegation from the Ministry of Commerce to meet with Mr. Phil Twyford, Deputy Minister of Commerce and Export of New Zealand and his colleagues via video. .
