Farmers wait to export longan directly to Chinese market as phytosanitary protocol enters into force
(Phnom Penh): Some longan farmers are ready to officially export Pailin longan fruit to the Chinese market, expecting that the market blockage will be solved. Meanwhile, some other farmers have been applying. Plantation at the General Department of Agriculture.
This preparation comes after the protocol on phytosanitary requirements for the export of fresh Pailin lentils from Cambodia to China came into force on March 16, 2022. On March 25, 2022, the Ministry of Agriculture informed the owners of Pailin longan plantations and the relevant private sector to apply for the registration of plantations and packaging plants as soon as possible in order to be able to export fresh longan to the Chinese market.
He said that currently about 60 farmers who are members of the association have registered the Good Agricultural Practices (GAP) at the Ministry of Agriculture, and some farmers are continuing to apply for the list.
According to Mr. Tom Sophoan, there are currently more than 4,000 hectares of Pailin lentils in Pailin province, of which 2,400 hectares are owned by community members, and more than 70% of the members who used to produce lentils are exported to neighboring countries. The standard is similar to GAP.
Mr. Chhim Vachira, Director of the Battambang Provincial Department of Agriculture, said that there are more than 6,000 hectares of Pailin Mien plantations in Battambang province. Following the announcement, some farmers were interested in applying for farm registration, while the provincial agriculture department continued to call on farmers to apply for registration at the Ministry of Agriculture so that it could be officially exported to China.
He said that in the future, when Pailin can be exported to China, it will be possible to solve the market shortage that farmers have faced in the past.
However, he observed that farmers still face technical challenges, especially after harvesting, horticulture, packaging has not yet been able to meet Chinese standards.
He added that so far, professional officers have trained about 400 farmers at the border on good agricultural practices, including about 300 mango and lentil farmers in Pailin according to Good Agricultural Standards (GAP).
According to the Director General of the General Department of Agriculture, at present, there is a cleaning, disinfection and packaging of Pailin longan for export, one location in Pailin province, while the other place is under construction in Sihanoukville. Thousands of hectares.
