Mr. Ky Sok Kim added that the development of skills, both technical and soft skills, will help our Cambodian workers and staff to get better jobs and better salaries, and be able to replace foreign workers and professionals. Factories will also reduce the cost of using foreign workers and skilled workers and increase the productivity and quality of their products.
Under the direction of Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Permanent Secretary of State and Mr. Phan Phalla, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, the Skills Development Fund provided $ 2.4 million for 78 training projects with trainees. A total of 5,581 people, including 43% women.
Mr. Ky Sok Kim encouraged factories and enterprises that need to develop human resources to cooperate with training institutions to prepare a request for funding to support the Skills Development Fund, but the Skills Development Fund will evaluate and select based on competitive principles, both quality and training costs.
At the same time, Mr. Kaing Monica, Deputy Secretary General of the Garment Association of Cambodia (GMAC), said that human resource issues are an indispensable issue and the Royal Government has recently launched a strategy to develop the garment sector. Shoes and bags, the first strategic point is to continue to strengthen human resources to improve factory productivity and create career paths for workers.
