Sri Lanka turns off street lighting nationwide, cuts off electricity for up to 13 hours a day
(Colombo): Sri Lanka has decided to turn off street lights to save electricity and increase power outages to 13 hours a day in the face of severe power shortages. This is according to the publication of Reuters on Thursday, March 31, 2022.
"We have already instructed our officials to turn off the lights throughout the country to save money, and the shipment of diesel under a $ 500 million loan from India is expected to arrive this weekend," she added. " But she warned that the situation was unlikely to improve any time soon, as authorities could only reduce power outages in the event of heavy rains in May so the blackouts could continue.
The island nation, which has a population of 22 million, is facing a shortage of electricity every day because the government does not have enough money to exchange foreign currency to buy electricity or fuel to start generators, and hydropower. More energy production. The Sri Lankan Ministry of Energy has also asked the government to allow some staff to work from home so that the ministry can control the country's access to fuel and energy.
