Samdech Techo Hun Sen: The construction of flyovers and subways in Phnom Penh has used the policy of taking the city or raising palm leaves and packing palm sugar
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, stated that the achievements of flyovers and subways built in Phnom Penh are due to the city's rent and land prices given to the private sector. Build to serve the city, which is a political use of the city to raise the city or take the palm leaves wrapped in palm sugar.
Samdech Techo's remarks were made at the inauguration ceremony of the Chom Chao flyover and subway this morning, Thursday, March 31, 2022, the fifth flyover in Phnom Penh.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated that currently Cambodia already has 5 flyovers, including: Kbal Thnal flyover, 7 Makara flyover, Lok Sang Hospital, Stung Meanchey flyover and subway, Stung Meanchey flyover and 5 Makara Techno subway And the Chom Chao flyover and subway and the sixth bridge are under construction.
Regarding the flyover and subway in Phnom Penh, Samdech Techo Hun Sen said, "In the past, these five flyovers were using the funds we received from the development of Koh Pich and the development of Chroy Changvar. "We are taking the rent price of the land given to the private sector to build the infrastructure to serve the city, which means that we have been using the method of taking the city to the city or take the palm leaves to pack brown sugar."
Samdech Techo further stated that there is nothing wrong with doing this, if not, is there any better option? And if those funds are diverted to the national budget and re-allocated, it will not be possible to provide opportunities for Phnom Penh to develop like this. At the same time, if you look at the construction of all the bridges are already spending millions of dollars.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen added that the construction is based on the actual needs of the people, if there is no need to increase the population, especially the increase of machinery, which is the means of the people. There is no need to do this.
Currently, the Royal Government is also in the process of constructing a flyover along National Road No. 5 at the intersection of the road connecting the Russey Keo cable-stayed bridge across the Tonle Sap River, which connects National Road No. 5 and the Chroy Changva satellite city.
Through flyovers and subways, travel has been facilitated, reducing traffic congestion, saving time and money. All these achievements show the strong commitment, effort and attention of the Royal Government led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen in solving the problems of the people as well as the development of the country.
On that occasion, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia advised all citizens who gather to enjoy the upcoming National New Year to continue to implement health rules, especially "3 do not and 3 prevent" measures to avoid transmission. Kovid 19 together.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also called on the people to enjoy the New Year, which has been interrupted for two years, so for 2022, but must try to maintain the social security gap to avoid Kovid 19 and so on.
In addition, Samdech Techo Hun Sen thanked the people who supported Samdech to provide an opportunity to remove the country from the flames of war and promote the development of the country in all fields.
Cambodia demands that the Special Rapporteur provide evidence on allegations that "human rights and democracy in Cambodia are intimidating"
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Cambodian Permanent Representative to the United Nations in Geneva, has asked the Special Rapporteur to provide concrete evidence on allegations that "human rights and democracy in Cambodia are intimidating and worrying." This is according to the statement received by Fresh News on March 31, 2022.
The Cambodian delegation made the remarks at an interactive dialogue on the Special Rapporteur's Oral Report on the Situation of Human Rights in Cambodia on March 29, 2022 in Geneva, with delegations from 36 countries. Participate in the intervention.
In response to the Special Rapporteur's allegations that human rights and democracy in Cambodia are intimidating and concerned, the Cambodian Ambassador and Permanent Representative to the United Nations in Geneva emphasized: Next:
* "Although Cambodia has provided its actual cooperation, which has been demonstrated through 19 dialogues with the Special Rapporteur since May 2021, the Special Rapporteur's report has not been disclosed. "Accurate and comprehensive, as well as skipping the principle of neutrality and non-discrimination."
"" Once again, the Special Rapporteur is requested to provide concrete evidence to substantiate the two allegations made by the Special Rapporteur in the past. "
* "The Dialogue on Human Rights is not about race and excellence on issues, but about long-distance running, which focuses on commitment, effort and real progress, and discussions on this topic. "It should be based on statistics and facts, not on perceptions."
* “Cambodia is a small country, but there are nearly 2,000 traditional and digital media outlets, including those that regularly criticize the government, nearly 6,000 unions and registered NGOs. Nearly 6,000.
* It is ridiculous that the Special Rapporteur insists on transparency and accountability, but instead criticizes the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations, which incorporates those critical principles into NGO funds and activities.
Regarding what the Special Rapporteur calls a large-scale trial, the Cambodian representative clarified:
* "The Special Rapporteur did not provide details on the facts involved in inciting disobedience to the military and civilians. Such acts are all condemned. "The mastermind of the plot was also condemned by a former Special Rapporteur on the Human Rights Council for inciting the overthrow of the popularly elected government."
* "The participation of any person with a political party and an NGO is not a license for that person to violate the law with impunity. "A crime is a crime and it can not be manipulated as a reason to serve another purpose."
Reminiscent of a meeting of opposition leaders who called those who re-applied for political rights traitors and incited bank borrowers not to repay their loans, the Cambodian representative questioned the Special Rapporteur. And the Western delegates present at the dialogue, "Are these all the voices against the freedom of expression you are advocating for in Cambodia?"
Finally, the Permanent Representative of Cambodia reaffirmed that Cambodia is highly committed to continuing to work with all partners to promote and protect human rights for all within the framework of the rule of law and to promote development. Sustainable and keep the peace in Cambodia, which is difficult to achieve and that all Cambodians value and support.
